Date: Fri, 13 Aug 93 2:39:11 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: investing

Karl Waldman:
> (the guy claims he can get an average of 25% per year, ....
> prediction of  economic growth rate:
> 	% inflation + change of GNP (not the new GDP) over the past year
> + Current accounts balance/GNP

Garbage, on both counts.  There are thousands of schemes that
attempt to predict economic growth, eg the "leading economic
indicators", "business cycles", etc.  None of them work very
well; for example each "business cycle" is different from the
last since the relationships between industries have changed,
and new industries have appear that become important to the
economy.  Many people have generated 25% return in the past;
many more have not; nobody can guaruntee anything close to
that in the future.  The lowest-risk return is c. 2.5%.  This
is based both on the historical real return of T-bills, widely
considered to be low risk in the past (but with mounting
debts, increasing unenforceability of tax laws, etc. that
may change), and the historical economic growth rate, 
which is what the average investor can expect as a real return.  
It is not coincidental that these two figures coincide, and
one should be highly dubious of any scheme that claims a
low-risk rate greater than 2.5% in the future.  (Not to
mention "risk-free returns" and other mythical creatures
of the investment world).  

> ... is this something "professional investors use"?

If they did, that information would already be reflected
in the market prices, and it wouldn't help you in the slightest.
Unless the information you have is _unique_ to a very small
(in total money) group of investors, and _important_ enough
to the security price or how it impacts your portfolio, it is
useless for finding good investments or avoiding bad ones.
If you don't have any important information to contribute
to the market, it (on average) won't contribute anything to
you.

Here are examples of information useful to investors:
* Your transaction costs:
	-- Bid/ask spreads
	-- Taxes (up to 40% of your capital gains!)
	-- Commissions, loads, wrap fees (often 3%/year!)

* The risk profile of your portfolio (diversification, probability of ruin)

* "Inside" information: unreleased earnings reports, planned
mergers or aquisitions, etc.  Inside-information laws are the 
speed limits of the investment world: if you want to get there
faster than the average driver, you best learn about the law
and how its enforced.  Like speeding laws, they are mostly enforced
in cases of blatant violation or when the market cops have some other 
motive for getting you, eg to "reign in uppity Jews" like Michael
Milken.

* Important trade secrets of small startup companies, and analysis
of their market consequences.

*  For long-term investing, insight into the business consequences of a
technology by someone uniquely well-versed in both the technology and
its market, eg betting on the success of spread-spectrum/CDMA.  I'm
not talking about a space fan betting on the success of nifty rocket XYZ!
We are talking here about people expert in the technology and the
market, who recognize the difference between what they hope and
what they can expect.

* Arbitrage opportunities: information reflected in one market
but not in another causally related market.  Mathematically precise
(or nearly so, there are theoretical nuances) relationships
exist between stocks and options on those stocks, between short-term
and long-term bonds with various coupon and call schemes, etc.
Computerized arbitrage based on these mathematial relationships
is a major industry.  New more subtle relationships are being discovered
all the time, and new "synthetic assets" are being constructed
out of options, futures, etc. all the time, math relationships
being worked out between them, and their trading automated.

Soft arbitrage includes things like being the first to hear
news about a flood that wiped out half the world's cocoa crop,
then going long on cocoa futures, for example.  There are many 
markets where arbitrage opportunity would seem possible, eg
the puzzling difference between the domestic-investor and
foreign-investor markets in the same companies on the Shanghai
exchange (Chinese nationals have to pay a >20% premium for
the same unit of stock ownership on many stocks!), but nobody
has figured out why the price difference exists with enough
confidence to do the arbitrage.  In this case, the threat
of confiscation of foreign shares by a future Communist 
crackdown may be a reason foreigners get a discount.  A
second explanation is that there is more domestic and
foreign demand, but then the difference should disappear
by arbitrage in Hong Kong markets.  The first people to
figure out the true reason for this price difference 
can make big money on it.

In nearly all these cases market noise is much greater than the
information being contributed to the market (one exception
is the example where half the world cocoa cropped
is wiped out!).  Many kinds of information may be less valuable
than the transaction cost if the amount of investment is
small or badly placed.  Thus investments are a gamble where
the edge is slightly with you, instead of slightly with the
house, as long as you are utilizing information that is
sufficiently unique and important enough to outweigh the
transaction costs.

