I wasn't able to get out until after midnight; I hear the rate
peaked at about 10:30pm out here in the Bay Area.  I saw a
fireball from the car while driving out, in the middle
of Silicon Valley metro light pollution & all.  I got far enough
south of Los Gatos in the foothills of the Santa Cruz mountains, so
that city glow was confined to <30 degrees of the northeast
sky.  Unfolded my lounge chair, wrapped myself in several sweaters and 
blankets, and just layed out staring at a very clear sky, nice for
viewing and daydreaming about comet mining, space colonies, 
Jupiter-sized brains, and the like.

There was no "storm" of meteors coming down at rates of
more than a few per minute, but the meteors were brighter
than I recall for previous Perseids.  I didn't keep count, but
guess about 40 bright meteors between 12:30-2:30 am PST, with over a 
dozen that left long trails that lasted for severeal seconds (on my 
retina or actually in the sky?), and a spectacular bursting fireball.
A nice show even without a "storm".

