From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: investing

Investment Performance of Dani Eder

At age 25 (27 July 1983) I had a net worth of zero.  This was a good sign, sincewhen I went to work for Boeing in October of 1981 I had a net worth of
minus ten thousand dollars (mostly school loans and a car loan, with
negligible assets).  At age 35 (two weeks ago) I have a net worth of $140,000.
About 50% of this is in 2 pieces of real estate - the place I live in and
a rental single family home in Seattle (which I am going to sell very soon).

Another big chunk is in company savings plans (401k type).  Financially I
have done best from the real estate.  I borrowed 106% of the purchase
price when I bought the rental house (90% from the seller and 16% as a
personal loan for the other 10% down and for remodeling costs).  For the
first few years I was paying more on the mortgage than I was collecting
in rent.  For the last few years the reverse has been true.  On net
my cash flow probably comes to zero over the life of the investment.
Today the property is worth twice the mortgage balance - mostly due to
a property value run-up in Seattle in the late 80's.

I have also done well by the company 401k plan, since Boeing matches
50 cents on the dollar for the first 8% of your salary that goes into
the plan (which is how much I put in).  There are few investments 
where you get a 50% bonus right at the start.  Within the 401k, the funds
I choose to invest in are a growth-type fund that has averaged about
12% over it's life, and a straight interest fund that pays 7.25% currently.
I expect the latter will drop next year (Boeing contracts with an
insurance company in advance to deposit this fund's money at a set rate.
Since the rate is set in advance for a full year, it does not reflect
this years' interest rate declines).  Thus I am looking at moving
money out of the 401k (and paying a penalty), in order to put it into
a higher yield investment.

One small but rewarding investment I made was a high-risk mortgage.
There was a company in town that specialized in arranging these deals.
A contractor/speculator needed money for a down payment and materials
on a HUD foreclosure that he wanted to fix up and re-sell.  He offered
1.75% per month for 3 month term, which I took.  The loan was secured
by a different property he owned which had a lower debt load.  I got
paid back, and he made money on the house.

One measure I use to see how well I am doing is to look at net worth vs
primary earnings.  My current net worth represents about 30% of the total
earnings I had over that period.  I'd like to keep it in that range or
better, and it is in that range because I follow investment rule #1:
spend less than you earn.


