Sean Merritt writes:
>If a professor had a thesis that he
>wished to advance and his numbers didn't jive(support his thesis) he
>might be tempted to "fudge" results. The danger of being called on the
>work is far less than that of someone in the physical sciences lets
>say. The reward is the list-o-pubs gets longer.

I am a PhD student in finance, so I think that I'm qualified to comment
on this.  I believe that the likelihood of "fudging" results is far lower
in empirical finance than in most kinds of physical sciences.  Why?

There are approximately 5 major data sets that we use... CRSP, Berkeley
options, ISSM, etc.  Every academic at every major institution has access
to the _exact same data_.  Most of the major results in empirical finance
can be, and have been, replicated and extended in a number of different
ways.

--Jimbo

