On a non-HAM, but general HEx related issue, I was wondering if any of the
new regulations would solve a particular problem I thought of. Take any
given reputation. Say you would just _love_ to see it devalued (maybe you're
really angry with me and would like to see HAM take a dive). All one would
have to do, if there are minimal BUY orders or none at the time, is simply
buy a couple shares, and sell very low! For instance, if a given reputation
is at p10, you could by one share, sell it at p1, and wipe it out.

Why doesn't this happen in real life? Well, everyone is working with money
in real life, so no-one is willing to take a hit like that I'm sure. Also,
while I understand the basics of how the NYSE and NASDAQ work, I am not sure
if there is anything necessarily precluding someone from doing that. But the
point is, in real life, even if it happened, the stock would instantly
stabilize, because the price generally reflects the health of the company,
and supply and demand takes over. This means that everyone but that one
seller still won't sell for any less than what they were a moment ago. ANd,
in the real world, since transactions are nearly constant, everything is
corrected very fast if this were to happen (again, assuming it could). On
HEx, though, the correction may not take place for a while.

How could this be fixed? A number of ways perhaps:

1. Increase the level of activity on HEx
2. Like with real markets, get rid of odd lots. This would mean a much larger
   loss to whomever tries to do this.
3. Place restrictions somehow.
4. Trust everyone not to do this.

Personally, I think #1 would be best. Has anyone else noticed this problem,
or has this actually happened? I know reputations have been artificially
inflated in some cases, but how about deflated?

